const items24 = [
  { id: "p2a-m19", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.4", difficulty: "easy", set: "p2a-s11",
    question: String.raw`During which time intervals is the net force on the cart zero?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$0$ to $2$ s and $4$ s to $7$ s`, String.raw`$2$ s to $4$ s and $7$ s to $9$ s`, String.raw`$4$ s to $7$ s only`, String.raw`The cart is never at zero net force.`],
    why: [String.raw`The net force is zero when the velocity is constant, which is when the graph is horizontal: $0$ to $2$ s and $4$ s to $7$ s. Constant velocity does not require the velocity to be zero.`,
      String.raw`These are the intervals when the velocity changes, so the net force is not zero.`,
      String.raw`The velocity is also constant from $0$ to $2$ s, at a smaller value. Constant velocity at any speed means zero net force.`,
      String.raw`The cart is moving the whole time, but moving at constant velocity does not require a net force.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m20", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.4", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s11",
    question: String.raw`Which statement describes the net force on the cart at $t = 8$ s?`,
    choices: ["It points left, because the velocity is decreasing.", "It points right, because the cart moves to the right.", "It is zero, because the cart's velocity is not zero.", "It points left, because the cart is moving backward."],
    why: [String.raw`The velocity is positive and its magnitude is decreasing, so the acceleration is negative. The net force has the same direction as the acceleration, to the left.`,
      "The net force points along the acceleration, not along the velocity. A cart that is slowing down has a net force opposite to its motion.",
      "The cart's velocity is changing at $8$ s, so the net force is not zero.",
      "The direction is right but the reason is not. The velocity stays positive: the cart is still moving to the right, only more slowly."], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m21", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.4", difficulty: "easy", set: "p2a-s12",
    question: String.raw`How does the tension $T$ in the upper string compare with the weight of the ball?`,
    choices: ["$T$ is greater than the weight, because only part of $T$ is vertical.", "$T$ equals the weight, because the ball is at rest and the net force is zero.", "$T$ is less than the weight, because the horizontal string also helps support the ball.", "$T$ equals $F$, because the ball has no acceleration in the horizontal direction."],
    why: [String.raw`Vertically, $T\cos 30^\circ = mg$, so $T = \dfrac{mg}{\cos 30^\circ} > mg$. The upper string must also supply the horizontal force $F$.`,
      "The ball is at rest, so the net force is zero, but the tension is slanted. Only its vertical component equals the weight.",
      "The horizontal string pulls sideways. It has no vertical component and does not hold up the ball.",
      "The horizontal component of $T$, $T\\sin 30^\\circ$, equals $F$, which is smaller than $T$."], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m22", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.4", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s12",
    question: String.raw`What is the magnitude of the horizontal force $F$?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$11.3$ N`, String.raw`$9.8$ N`, String.raw`$17.0$ N`, String.raw`$33.9$ N`],
    why: [String.raw`The ball is in equilibrium. Vertically $T\cos 30^\circ = mg$, and horizontally $T\sin 30^\circ = F$. Dividing, $F = mg\tan 30^\circ = (2.0)(9.8)(0.577) = 11.3$ N.`,
      String.raw`$9.8$ N is $mg\sin 30^\circ$, which uses the weight with the wrong trigonometric function and no tension.`,
      String.raw`$17.0$ N is $mg\cos 30^\circ$, which is not a component that appears in either equilibrium equation.`,
      String.raw`$33.9$ N is $\dfrac{mg}{\tan 30^\circ}$, which uses the tangent upside down.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m23", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.4", difficulty: "difficult", set: "p2a-s13",
    question: String.raw`What is the magnitude of $F$?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$20.6$ N`, String.raw`$16.9$ N`, String.raw`$24.1$ N`, String.raw`$42.0$ N`],
    why: [String.raw`The block is at rest, so the forces along the incline cancel. The weight's component down the slope is $mg\sin 35^\circ$. The horizontal force's component up the slope is $F\cos 35^\circ$. Setting them equal, $F = mg\tan 35^\circ = (3.0)(9.8)(0.700) = 20.6$ N.`,
      String.raw`$16.9$ N is $mg\sin 35^\circ$, which treats $F$ as if it were directed along the incline.`,
      String.raw`$24.1$ N is $mg\cos 35^\circ$, which is the normal force's share of the weight, not the force along the slope.`,
      String.raw`$42.0$ N is $\dfrac{mg}{\tan 35^\circ}$, which uses the tangent upside down.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m24", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.4", difficulty: "moderate",
    stem: String.raw`A passenger standing in a bus leans forward when the bus brakes.`,
    question: String.raw`Which explanation is consistent with Newton's first law?`,
    choices: ["No forward force acts, and the body tends to keep its velocity.", "A forward force remains on the passenger from the bus's earlier motion.", "The passenger pushes forward on the bus with a force equal to the braking force.", "Gravity pulls the passenger forward as the bus slows down on the road."],
    why: [String.raw`With no net force, an object keeps its velocity. The bus slows, but the passenger's upper body continues forward until a force, such as the floor's friction on the feet or a handrail, changes its velocity.`,
      "A force is an interaction, not something stored in an object from earlier motion.",
      "The passenger does not push the bus to brake it, and the braking force acts on the bus. No force is exerted on the passenger in the forward direction.",
      "Gravity points down. It has no forward horizontal component on level ground."], answer: 0 },
];

