const items25 = [
  { id: "p2a-m25", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s14",
    question: String.raw`Which graph shows the net force on the cart as a function of time?`,
    choices: ["Graph C", "Graph A", "Graph B", "Graph D"],
    why: [String.raw`The net force is $ma$, so it follows the slope of the velocity graph. From $0$ to $3$ s the slope is $2$ m/s² and $F = (1.5)(2) = 3.0$ N. From $3$ to $5$ s the slope is zero and $F = 0$. From $5$ to $7$ s the slope is $-3$ m/s² and $F = -4.5$ N.`,
      "Graph A has the same shape as the velocity graph. The force is proportional to the slope of the velocity graph, not to the velocity.",
      "Graph B has the right size but the wrong sign after $5$ s. The cart is slowing down, so the net force points opposite to its velocity.",
      "Graph D shows a force of $3.0$ N while the velocity is constant. Constant velocity means zero net force, even though the cart is moving."], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m26", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "easy", set: "p2a-s14",
    question: String.raw`A second cart of mass $2.0$ kg has the same velocity graph. What is the net force on it at $t = 2.0$ s?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$4.0$ N`, String.raw`$8.0$ N`, String.raw`$3.0$ N`, String.raw`$2.0$ N`],
    why: [String.raw`The acceleration at $2.0$ s is the slope, $2.0$ m/s². Then $F = ma = (2.0)(2.0) = 4.0$ N.`,
      String.raw`$8.0$ N uses the velocity at $2.0$ s, $4.0$ m/s, in place of the acceleration.`,
      String.raw`$3.0$ N is the net force on the $1.5$ kg cart. The force changes with the mass.`,
      String.raw`$2.0$ N is the acceleration with the mass left out.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m27", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s15",
    question: String.raw`What is the tension in the chain between the two loads?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$m_2(g + a)$`, String.raw`$m_2(g - a)$`, String.raw`$m_2 a$`, String.raw`$(m_1 + m_2)(g + a)$`],
    why: [String.raw`Apply Newton's second law to $m_2$, with up as positive: $T - m_2 g = m_2 a$, so $T = m_2(g + a)$.`,
      String.raw`$m_2(g - a)$ has the sign of $a$ reversed. The load accelerates upward, so the chain must pull with more than the weight.`,
      String.raw`$m_2 a$ leaves out the weight. The chain supports the weight and also accelerates the load.`,
      String.raw`$(m_1 + m_2)(g + a)$ is the tension in the cable, which also supports $m_1$.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m28", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s15",
    question: String.raw`What is the tension in the crane cable?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$(m_1 + m_2)(g + a)$`, String.raw`$m_1(g + a)$`, String.raw`$(m_1 + m_2)(g - a)$`, String.raw`$(m_1 + m_2)g + m_1 a$`],
    why: [String.raw`Take both loads as one system of mass $m_1 + m_2$. The chain is internal, and the only external vertical forces are the cable and the weights: $T - (m_1 + m_2)g = (m_1 + m_2)a$.`,
      String.raw`$m_1(g + a)$ counts only the upper load. The cable also supports $m_2$.`,
      String.raw`$(m_1 + m_2)(g - a)$ has the sign of $a$ reversed.`,
      String.raw`$(m_1 + m_2)g + m_1 a$ accelerates only $m_1$. Both loads accelerate upward.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m29", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s16",
    question: String.raw`What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the blocks?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$1.96$ m/s²`, String.raw`$3.27$ m/s²`, String.raw`$3.92$ m/s²`, String.raw`$4.90$ m/s²`],
    why: [String.raw`For the two blocks as one system, the net force is $(0.60 - 0.40)(9.8) = 1.96$ N and the total mass is $1.00$ kg, so $a = 1.96$ m/s².`,
      String.raw`$3.27$ m/s² divides the net force by $0.60$ kg only.`,
      String.raw`$3.92$ m/s² divides the net force by the average mass, $0.50$ kg, instead of the total mass.`,
      String.raw`$4.90$ m/s² divides the net force by $0.40$ kg only.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m30", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "difficult", set: "p2a-s16",
    question: String.raw`What does the spring scale read while the blocks are accelerating?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$9.4$ N`, String.raw`$9.8$ N`, String.raw`$4.7$ N`, String.raw`$5.9$ N`],
    why: [String.raw`For the $0.40$ kg block, $T - (0.40)(9.8) = (0.40)(1.96)$, so $T = 4.70$ N. The string pulls down on the pulley with $T$ on each side, so the scale carries $2T = 9.4$ N, a little less than the total weight.`,
      String.raw`$9.8$ N is the total weight, which is the reading only if the blocks are not accelerating.`,
      String.raw`$4.7$ N is the tension $T$. The string pulls on the pulley on both sides, so the scale reads $2T$.`,
      String.raw`$5.9$ N is the weight of the $0.60$ kg block alone.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m31", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s17",
    question: String.raw`What is the tension in the rope at $t = 1.0$ s?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$84.4$ N`, String.raw`$78.4$ N`, String.raw`$72.4$ N`, String.raw`$6.0$ N`],
    why: [String.raw`From $0$ to $2$ s the acceleration is $\dfrac{1.5}{2.0} = 0.75$ m/s² upward. Then $T - mg = ma$ gives $T = (8.0)(9.8 + 0.75) = 84.4$ N.`,
      String.raw`$78.4$ N is the weight, which is the tension only when the bucket moves at constant velocity.`,
      String.raw`$72.4$ N subtracts $ma$ from the weight. The bucket accelerates upward, so the tension is larger than the weight.`,
      String.raw`$6.0$ N is $ma$ alone. The rope also supports the weight.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m32", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "easy", set: "p2a-s17",
    question: String.raw`During which interval is the tension in the rope smallest?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$6$ s to $8$ s`, String.raw`$0$ s to $2$ s`, String.raw`$2$ s to $6$ s`, "The tension is always the same."],
    why: [String.raw`From $6$ to $8$ s the bucket is moving up and slowing down, so the acceleration is downward and $T = m(g - a)$ is less than the weight.`,
      String.raw`From $0$ to $2$ s the acceleration is upward, so the tension is greater than the weight.`,
      String.raw`From $2$ to $6$ s the velocity is constant, so the tension equals the weight, which is larger than the tension while slowing.`,
      String.raw`The bucket moves upward the whole time, but the tension depends on the acceleration, not the direction of motion.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m33", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s18",
    question: String.raw`What is the magnitude of the puck's acceleration?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$3.3$ m/s²`, String.raw`$4.7$ m/s²`, String.raw`$0.67$ m/s²`, String.raw`$2.7$ m/s²`],
    why: [String.raw`The forces are perpendicular, so the net force is $\sqrt{6.0^2 + 8.0^2} = 10$ N, and $a = \dfrac{10}{3.0} = 3.3$ m/s².`,
      String.raw`$4.7$ m/s² adds the magnitudes, $\dfrac{6.0 + 8.0}{3.0}$. Forces add as vectors.`,
      String.raw`$0.67$ m/s² subtracts the magnitudes, $\dfrac{8.0 - 6.0}{3.0}$, as if the forces were opposite.`,
      String.raw`$2.7$ m/s² uses the $8.0$ N force alone.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m34", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "easy", set: "p2a-s18",
    question: String.raw`In which direction does the puck accelerate?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$53^\circ$ north of east`, String.raw`$37^\circ$ north of east`, String.raw`$45^\circ$ north of east`, String.raw`$53^\circ$ east of north`],
    why: [String.raw`The acceleration points along the net force. The angle north of east satisfies $\tan\theta = \dfrac{8.0}{6.0}$, so $\theta = 53^\circ$.`,
      String.raw`$37^\circ$ comes from $\tan\theta = \dfrac{6.0}{8.0}$, which is the angle from north.`,
      String.raw`$45^\circ$ would need two forces of equal magnitude.`,
      String.raw`$53^\circ$ east of north measures the angle from the wrong axis. It is the same direction as $37^\circ$ north of east.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m35", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s19",
    question: String.raw`What is the mass of the cart?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$0.50$ kg`, String.raw`$2.0$ kg`, String.raw`$0.69$ kg`, String.raw`$1.0$ kg`],
    why: [String.raw`The slope of $a$ versus $F$ is $\dfrac{1}{m}$. Using two points on the line, $(1.0, 0)$ and $(3.0, 4.0)$, the slope is $2.0$ kg⁻¹, so $m = 0.50$ kg.`,
      String.raw`$2.0$ kg reads the slope as the mass. The slope of $a$ versus $F$ is the reciprocal of the mass.`,
      String.raw`$0.69$ kg is $\dfrac{F}{a}$ at the last data point, $\dfrac{3.5}{5.1}$. That ratio is not the mass, because the line does not pass through the origin.`,
      String.raw`$1.0$ kg is the intercept on the force axis, which is a force in newtons, not a mass.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m36", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s19",
    question: String.raw`Which statement explains why the line crosses the force axis at $1.0$ N instead of at the origin?`,
    choices: ["A $1.0$ N force opposes the applied force.", "The cart has a mass of $1.0$ kg, which sets the intercept.", "The cart would accelerate at $1.0$ m/s² with no applied force.", "The net force on the cart is $1.0$ N when the applied force is zero."],
    why: [String.raw`The acceleration is zero when the net force is zero, so an applied force of $1.0$ N must be balanced by an opposing force, such as friction. Then $a = \dfrac{F - 1.0}{m}$.`,
      "The mass is found from the slope, not the intercept. The intercept is a force.",
      "At $F = 0$ the line extended gives a negative acceleration, not $1.0$ m/s².",
      "With no applied force, the line gives a negative acceleration: the opposing force would slow the cart, so the net force is not $1.0$ N in the direction of $F$."], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m37", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate",
    stem: String.raw`A $1500$ kg car starts from rest and travels $100$ m along a straight, level road in $8.0$ s with constant acceleration.`,
    question: String.raw`What is the magnitude of the net horizontal force on the car?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$4.7 \times 10^{3}$ N`, String.raw`$2.3 \times 10^{3}$ N`, String.raw`$1.9 \times 10^{4}$ N`, String.raw`$3.8 \times 10^{4}$ N`],
    why: [String.raw`Starting from rest, $x = \tfrac{1}{2}at^2$, so $a = \dfrac{2(100)}{(8.0)^2} = 3.125$ m/s². Then $F = ma = (1500)(3.125) = 4.7 \times 10^{3}$ N.`,
      String.raw`$2.3 \times 10^{3}$ N uses $a = \dfrac{x}{t^2}$ and leaves out the factor of $2$.`,
      String.raw`$1.9 \times 10^{4}$ N uses the average speed, $\dfrac{x}{t} = 12.5$ m/s, as if it were the acceleration.`,
      String.raw`$3.8 \times 10^{4}$ N is $mv$ for the final speed $25$ m/s, which is not a force.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m38", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s20",
    question: String.raw`Which statement describes the block at this instant?`,
    choices: ["It accelerates at $2.3$ m/s² downward and may move up or down.", "It accelerates at $2.3$ m/s² downward and must be moving down.", "It accelerates at $7.5$ m/s² upward and must be moving up.", "It accelerates at $2.3$ m/s² upward and may move up or down."],
    why: [String.raw`The weight is $(2.0)(9.8) = 19.6$ N and the tension is $15$ N, so the net force is $4.6$ N downward and $a = \dfrac{4.6}{2.0} = 2.3$ m/s² downward. The acceleration does not fix the direction of the velocity: the block could be moving down and speeding up, or moving up and slowing down.`,
      "A downward acceleration does not require downward motion. A block moving upward and slowing down has the same acceleration.",
      String.raw`$7.5$ m/s² is $\dfrac{T}{m}$, which leaves out the weight. The tension is smaller than the weight, so the acceleration is downward.`,
      "The tension is smaller than the weight, so the net force and acceleration point down, not up."], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m39", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s21",
    question: String.raw`Which graph shows the velocity of the sled?`,
    choices: ["Graph C", "Graph A", "Graph B", "Graph D"],
    why: [String.raw`From $0$ to $4$ s, $a = \dfrac{6.0}{2.0} = 3.0$ m/s², so the velocity rises to $12$ m/s. After the rope is released, the net force is zero, so the velocity stays at $12$ m/s.`,
      "Graph A shows the sled slowing down after the rope is released. With no friction, no force acts to slow it.",
      "Graph B shows the velocity dropping to zero at once. A change in velocity requires time under a force.",
      "Graph D shows the sled still speeding up after the rope is released. Without a force, there is no acceleration."], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m40", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "easy",
    stem: String.raw`A ball is thrown straight up. Air resistance is negligible. At the highest point of its path, the ball is momentarily at rest.`,
    question: String.raw`What is the net force on the ball at that instant?`,
    choices: ["The ball's weight, directed downward", "Zero, because the ball is at rest", "The ball's weight, directed upward", "Zero, because the forces cancel at the top"],
    why: [String.raw`Only gravity acts on the ball. The net force is the weight, downward, and the acceleration is $g$ throughout the flight, including at the top.`,
      "The velocity is zero at that instant, but the velocity is changing. A zero velocity does not mean a zero net force.",
      "The weight always points downward. The ball's earlier upward motion does not reverse it.",
      "No second force acts on the ball to cancel the weight."], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m41", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "easy", set: "p2a-s22",
    question: String.raw`What is the acceleration of the crate?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$2.0$ m/s² upward`, String.raw`$2.0$ m/s² downward`, String.raw`$11.8$ m/s² upward`, String.raw`$21.6$ m/s² upward`],
    why: [String.raw`The weight is $(500)(9.8) = 4900$ N, so the net force is $5900 - 4900 = 1000$ N upward and $a = \dfrac{1000}{500} = 2.0$ m/s² upward.`,
      "The tension is larger than the weight, so the net force and the acceleration point up.",
      String.raw`$11.8$ m/s² is $\dfrac{T}{m}$, which leaves out the weight.`,
      String.raw`$21.6$ m/s² is $\dfrac{T + mg}{m}$, which adds the weight when it should be subtracted.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m42", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate", set: "p2a-s23",
    question: String.raw`What is the magnitude of the net force on the object?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$8.0$ N`, String.raw`$12$ N`, String.raw`$4.0$ N`, String.raw`$36$ N`],
    why: [String.raw`The object starts from rest, so $x = \tfrac{1}{2}at^2$. At $t = 3.0$ s, $x = 9.0$ m, so $a = \dfrac{2(9.0)}{(3.0)^2} = 2.0$ m/s² and $F = (4.0)(2.0) = 8.0$ N.`,
      String.raw`$12$ N uses $\dfrac{x}{t} = 3.0$ m/s as the acceleration.`,
      String.raw`$4.0$ N uses $\dfrac{x}{t^2}$ as the acceleration and leaves out the factor of $2$.`,
      String.raw`$36$ N is the mass times the position, $(4.0)(9.0)$.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m43", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "easy",
    stem: String.raw`A net force $F$ acting on an object of mass $m$ gives it an acceleration $a$.`,
    question: String.raw`A net force of $3F$ acts on an object of mass $2m$. What is the acceleration?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$1.5a$`, String.raw`$6a$`, String.raw`$3a$`, String.raw`$\dfrac{2}{3}a$`],
    why: [String.raw`$a' = \dfrac{3F}{2m} = 1.5\,\dfrac{F}{m} = 1.5a$.`,
      String.raw`$6a$ multiplies by the mass factor instead of dividing by it.`,
      String.raw`$3a$ accounts for the force and leaves out the larger mass.`,
      String.raw`$\dfrac{2}{3}a$ is the reciprocal of the correct factor.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m44", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate",
    stem: String.raw`A ball is thrown straight up. Air resistance is not negligible. The ball passes a point on the way up and again, later, on the way down.`,
    question: String.raw`How does the magnitude of the ball's acceleration at that point going up compare with the magnitude going down?`,
    choices: ["Greater going up, because drag and weight both point down then.", "Greater going down, because the ball has had more time to speed up.", "The same, because the weight of the ball does not change between the two times.", "The same, because the ball passes the same height going up and going down."],
    why: [String.raw`Going up, the weight and the air resistance both point down, so $a = g + \dfrac{f}{m}$. Going down, air resistance points up, so $a = g - \dfrac{f}{m}$.`,
      "The acceleration depends on the net force at that instant, not on how long the ball has been falling.",
      "The weight is the same, but the air resistance changes direction, so the net force does not.",
      "The height does not set the net force. Air resistance depends on the direction of motion, which is different."], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m45", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "difficult", set: "p2a-s24",
    question: String.raw`What is the tension in the cord while the blocks accelerate?`,
    choices: [String.raw`$1.57$ N`, String.raw`$1.96$ N`, String.raw`$0.39$ N`, String.raw`$7.84$ N`],
    why: [String.raw`Treat the two blocks as one system: $a = \dfrac{(0.20)(9.8)}{1.00} = 1.96$ m/s². The tension is the only horizontal force on the table block, so $T = Ma = (0.80)(1.96) = 1.57$ N.`,
      String.raw`$1.96$ N is the weight of the hanging block, which would be the tension only if the blocks were not accelerating.`,
      String.raw`$0.39$ N is the hanging block's mass times the acceleration, $(0.20)(1.96)$, which is the net force on it, not the tension.`,
      String.raw`$7.84$ N is the weight of the table block, which does not pull along the cord.`], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m46", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "difficult", set: "p2a-s25",
    question: String.raw`Which ranking of the magnitudes of the accelerations of the carts is correct?`,
    choices: ["B > A > C = D", "B > D > A > C", "B > A = D > C", "C > A > B = D"],
    why: [String.raw`$a = \dfrac{F_{net}}{m}$ gives $A: \dfrac{4}{2.0} = 2.0$, $B: \dfrac{9}{3.0} = 3.0$, $C: \dfrac{1}{1.0} = 1.0$, $D: \dfrac{4}{4.0} = 1.0$ m/s². So $B > A > C = D$.`,
      "B > D > A > C ranks by the largest single force, $9, 8, 6, 5$ N. Acceleration depends on the net force and the mass.",
      "B > A = D > C ranks by net force, $9, 4, 4, 1$ N, and leaves out the different masses.",
      "C > A > B = D adds the two forces instead of subtracting them."], answer: 0 },
  { id: "p2a-m47", type: "mcq", skill: "u2", topic: "2.5", difficulty: "moderate",
    stem: String.raw`An elevator is moving downward and slowing to a stop at a floor.`,
    question: String.raw`How does the tension in the elevator's cable compare with the elevator's weight?`,
    choices: ["Greater, because the acceleration is upward.", "Less, because the elevator is moving downward at that time.", "Equal, because the elevator is nearly at rest at that time.", "Less, because the acceleration of the elevator is downward."],
    why: [String.raw`The velocity is downward and its magnitude is decreasing, so the acceleration is upward. The net force is upward, so $T - mg = ma$ gives $T > mg$.`,
      "The direction of motion does not set the direction of the net force. The acceleration does.",
      "The elevator is slowing, so its velocity changes, and the net force is not zero.",
      "The acceleration points opposite to the velocity when an object slows down, so it is upward here."], answer: 0 },
];

